[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Front-running and MEV Mitigation in Blockchain"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Maximal Extractable Value (MEV) refers to the maximum value that can be extracted from block production in excess of the standard block reward and gas fees by including, excluding, or changing the order of transactions in a block. Front-running is a prominent subset of MEV where an actor observes a pending transaction in the public mempool and inserts their own transaction ahead of it to profit."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Threat Model"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"In public blockchains like Ethereum, transactions reside in a peer-to-peer mempool before being included in a block. Searchers and bots continuously scan this mempool for profitable opportunities, such as decentralized exchange (DEX) arbitrage, liquidations, and large swaps that cause significant price slippage (sandwich attacks)."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Sandwich Attacks: A bot detects a large buy order, places a buy order with a higher gas price just before it, and places a sell order immediately after to profit from the artificially inflated price."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Arbitrage: Bots exploit price discrepancies across different liquidity pools by front-running pending user trades."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Liquidation Front-running: Bots race to liquidate undercollateralized loans to capture the liquidation bonus."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"MEV Mitigation Strategies"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Several architectural patterns and cryptographic primitives have emerged to protect users from front-running and harmful MEV extraction."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Private RPC Endpoints: Routing transactions directly to validators through services like Flashbots Protect bypasses the public mempool entirely."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Commit-Reveal Schemes: Users submit a cryptographic hash of their transaction first, and reveal the parameters in a subsequent block, preventing bots from inspecting the payload in the mempool."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Slippage Tolerance: Setting tight slippage bounds in smart contract function calls ensures transactions revert if front-running pushes the execution price beyond acceptable limits."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing Slippage Protection in Solidity"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Below is an example of a vulnerable token swap function compared to a secure implementation that enforces a minimum output amount to mitigate sandwich attacks."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"contract SecureSwap {
    function swapExactTokensForTokens(
        uint amountIn,
        uint amountOutMin,
        address[] calldata path,
        address to,
        uint deadline
    ) external returns (uint[] memory amounts) {
        require(block.timestamp <= deadline, "Transaction expired");
        // Ensure the output meets the minimum threshold to prevent sandwich attacks
        amounts = router.swapExactTokensForTokens(amountIn, amountOutMin, path, to, deadline);
        require(amounts[amounts.length - 1] >= amountOutMin, "Insufficient output amount");
    }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mitigating MEV and front-running requires a multi-layered approach involving smart contract design (e.g., strict slippage limits), infrastructure tools (e.g., private mempools), and emerging consensus mechanisms like Proposer-Builder Separation (PBS). Developers must prioritize these defenses to protect end-users from value extraction."}]
